## 4. One dataset should grow with the learner

Take D09 IoT & Sensors, which we will use for both intermediate and advanced learning.

D09 — IoT & Sensor Telemetry

devices · sensor_readings · device_locations · alerts · maintenance_events

Learn — L042: Flatten Complex Event Payloads

Read a small nested JSON sensor event. Extract device ID, temperature and event timestamp.

Practice — Intermediate

Parse sensor events with missing fields, malformed timestamps, and repeated device readings. Return a validated DataFrame.

Tools — Streaming & Performance

Replay sensor batches, inspect event-time windows, examine late arrivals and evaluate a stateful query.

Project P10 — IoT Monitoring & Failure Recovery

Build an end-to-end sensor monitoring pipeline with anomaly rules, incident outputs, checkpoints, and restart validation.

We should do this for each dataset. The beginner version is intentionally small, while the project version is realistic enough to reveal engineering problems.

And this should happen without presenting 10 million rows to someone learning `select()` for the first time.

## 5. The common content contract

Before creating more content, we should define a shared structure that connects everything.

A lesson should know which dataset it uses, which concepts it teaches, which questions reinforce those concepts, and which projects require them.

For example:

```
lesson:
  id: L025
  title: Inner and Outer Joins
  dataset: D01
  dataset_version: "1.0"
  tables: [customers, orders]
  concepts: [inner_join, left_join, full_join]
  tools: [playground, spark_sql_lab]
  practice: [Q0121, Q0122, Q0123, Q0124, Q0125]
  related_projects: [P01]
```

We would create equivalent references for practice questions, project milestones, and tools. One shared registry becomes the source of truth, instead of manually duplicating relationships in several parts of the application.

The 450 questions also need meaningful variation. We should avoid forcing five artificial questions onto a narrow concept merely because the mapping assigns five slots. We can preserve the overall target while redistributing questions to concepts that deserve more coverage.

## 6. How the learner navigates runtime.school

The platform should preserve its familiar Learn, Practice, Projects, and Tools navigation.

| Learner opens | What they should immediately understand                                                  |
| ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Learn         | What am I learning, why does it matter, and what data am I using?                        |
| Practice      | What problem do I need to solve, what tables are available, and what output is expected? |
| Projects      | What am I building, what do I already need to know, and what will I deliver?             |
| Tools         | What can I experiment with, and how does it relate to the concepts I've learned?         |
| Datasets      | What does this dataset represent, which tables relate, and where is it used?             |

I would also put contextual navigation inside each lesson and project. If a learner is struggling with a join question, they should be able to reopen the relevant lesson. If they are working on a project, they should see the prerequisite lessons and practice problems without searching through the entire curriculum.

Returning learners should have a prominent Continue action, while Learn, Practice, and Projects remain independently browsable.

## 7. What we need to validate before building all the content

A real PySpark environment introduces infrastructure requirements that our existing SQL and Python tracks may not have.

We should run actual PySpark on an isolated backend, not pretend a browser-only Python interpreter is Spark. Beginner exercises can run on constrained local-mode Spark workers. Delta, streaming, and optimization work will need dedicated execution modes and stronger state isolation.

Structured Streaming, for example, uses checkpoints to recover query progress and state; those behaviors have to be tested using controlled event sequences rather than displaying a static table and calling it streaming.&#x20;

