The content also needs these quality gates:

| Validation           | Required condition                                                            |
| -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Dataset consistency  | Same entity definitions and business rules across lessons, practice, projects |
| Concept progression  | Prerequisites introduced before they're required                              |
| Question correctness | Reference solutions and hidden tests pass                                     |
| Tool integration     | The tool can actually execute the operation being taught                      |
| Project feasibility  | Every milestone is supported by available datasets and runtimes               |
| Learning clarity     | Learner can understand the task without guessing missing business rules       |
| Runtime isolation    | One learner's execution cannot affect another learner's environment           |

## 8. The build order I'd use

01

Lock dataset contracts

Finalize tables, relationships, licensing, formats, business rules and fixture versions across all 15 domains.

02

Build the curriculum dependency map

Assign prerequisites, dataset views, questions, tool activities and projects to every lesson.

03

Implement one complete learning path

Use D01 Retail: lessons → practice → Playground/SQL Lab → P01 project with real grading.

04

Validate all execution modes

Prove DataFrame, Delta, streaming and performance grading work before relying on them.

05

Expand the full curriculum

Produce the remaining lessons, questions and projects against the validated system.

The most important principle for runtime.school is this:

Datasets are the shared learning environment. Lessons teach skills using that environment. Practice tests those skills independently. Projects combine them into real engineering work. Tools help learners explore, understand, and improve their solutions.

I would treat that as a non-negotiable requirement across PySpark and our future Power BI, Machine Learning, Data Engineering, and AI engineering tracks, while adapting the tools and project formats to each subject.